  "@type": "Article",
  "headline": "Why isn't my drywall turning out smooth?",
  "author": {
    "@type": "Organization",
    "name": "HH Remodeling LLC"
  },
  "publisher": {
    "@type": "Organization",
    "name": "HH Remodeling LLC",
    "url": "http://coloradoremodeling.company"
  },
  "articleBody": "It's super frustrating when you're trying to get a perfect drywall finish and it just isn't happening, right? Often, the culprit is rushing. Drywall mud needs time to dry between coats—we're talking about 24 hours for that first coat, depending on the temperature and humidity. Trying to apply the next layer too soon can lead to all sorts of issues, like bubbling or uneven surfaces, because the underlying layer isn't fully set. Another common mistake is leaving too much joint compound on the wall or not feathering the edges properly. This creates noticeable ridges and bumps that no amount of sanding can truly fix without redoing the work. And visible tape joints? That's usually from not embedding the tape properly or not applying enough mud over it. It’s all about patience and a good eye for detail. If you're finding it consistently tough to get that smooth, professional look, it might be time to bring in someone with more experience. We're here to help folks in Denver and the surrounding areas achieve that flawless finish.",
